{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And the elders of Moab went.</b> In the chapter חלק it was taught: Moab and Midian were never at peace with each other. A parable: two dogs that were in a flock and hated each other, and a wolf came upon one of them. [The other] said: If I do not help him, tomorrow he will come upon me. They both went and killed the wolf. Rav Mesharshiya said: this is what people say: שונרא וכרכושתא — meaning, a cat and a marten — did they make a pact, [only] because of the evil rodent. [i.e., עבדו הלולא מתרבא דביש גדא — they acted together only because of the bad mouse.]" "<b>And divinations in their hand.</b> The wages of divination were in their hand, and so the Jerusalem Targum: ואגרתא דקסמיא בידיהון. Or alternatively: אגרתא דקסמיא בידיהון — that they brought him books of diviners."], :book :numbers, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "The Aramaic proverb at the end is rendered hyper-literally; the phrase עבדו הלולא מתרבא דביש גדא is left in transliteration with the suggested explanation woven in, as the Hebrew note itself glosses the preceding Aramaic."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:09:58.122510648Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}